<%!-- [LEGAL REVIEW] Draft privacy notice, written from what the app
     actually stores (see the contexts named in comments). Placeholders in
     [BRACKETS] must be filled in. Keep it true when features change. --%>
<Layouts.app flash={@flash} current_scope={@current_scope} shell={:marketing}>
  <article
    id="privacy"
    class="mx-auto flex max-w-2xl flex-col gap-7 px-4 py-14 text-[15px] leading-relaxed sm:px-8"
  >
    <header class="flex flex-col gap-2">
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl font-bold">Privacy</h1>
      <p class="text-ks-muted">Last updated {@updated}</p>
      <p
        id="privacy-review-note"
        class="rounded-box border border-accent/40 bg-ks-amber-soft px-4 py-3 text-sm text-ks-amber-deep"
      >
        [LEGAL REVIEW] This notice is a working draft and will be reviewed before launch.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section id="privacy-who" class="flex flex-col gap-2">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Who is responsible</h2>
      <p>
        [COMPANY NAME], [ADDRESS], is responsible for the personal data described here. Contact:
        [CONTACT EMAIL]. [LEGAL REVIEW: name a Data Protection Officer if required under the NDPA.]
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="privacy-what" class="flex flex-col gap-2">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">What we keep, and why</h2>
      <ul class="flex list-disc flex-col gap-1.5 pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong>Your account:</strong> name, email, password (stored only as a one-way hash),
          optional photo and bio, your settings, and when you agreed to the terms. To run your account.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your albums:</strong> photos and their details (titles, dates, captions, places,
          people's names you type). Location data embedded in photos is removed from the copies
          guests see. To show your albums to you and the people you share with.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Security:</strong>
          access codes and two-step login secrets are stored encrypted. We
          record the devices you're signed in on and limit repeated wrong codes and passwords, using
          the network address they came from.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Guests:</strong>
          guests don't need an account. When a guest adds a photo or signs a
          guestbook we keep the name they give, and a random identifier for their browser so a heart
          counts once. We count how often an album is opened, not who opened it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Payments:</strong>
          Flutterwave processes card payments. We never see your full card
          number; we keep a reference Flutterwave gives us for renewals, the card's brand and last four
          digits, and your invoices.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Support:</strong> messages and attachments you send us.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Our legal bases are providing the service you signed up for, our legitimate interest in keeping
        it safe, and the law (for example keeping invoices). [LEGAL REVIEW: confirm bases under the NDPA.]
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="privacy-processors" class="flex flex-col gap-2">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Who helps us</h2>
      <p>
        We use a few providers who handle data on our behalf: Cloudflare (photo storage, R2),
        Resend (sending email), Flutterwave (payments) and DigitalOcean (our servers). Some of them store
        data outside Nigeria. [LEGAL REVIEW: cross-border transfer safeguards.]
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="privacy-cookies" class="flex flex-col gap-2">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Cookies</h2>
      <p>
        We use one cookie to keep you signed in and to remember which shared albums you've unlocked, plus
        your browser's local storage for your light or dark theme. No advertising or tracking cookies.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="privacy-retention" class="flex flex-col gap-2">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">How long we keep it</h2>
      <p>
        For as long as you have an account. When you delete it, everything goes after a 14-day window in
        which you can change your mind, except invoices and payment records, which we keep for
        [RETENTION PERIOD] for accounting. Exports you request are deleted after 7 days.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="privacy-rights" class="flex flex-col gap-2">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        Under Nigeria's Data Protection Act 2023 you can ask to see, correct, move or delete your data,
        and object to how we use it. Most of this you can do yourself: edit your details in Settings,
        export everything from Settings → Data &amp; privacy, or delete your account there. For anything
        else, write to [CONTACT EMAIL]. You can also complain to the Nigeria Data Protection Commission.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="privacy-changes" class="flex flex-col gap-2">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Changes</h2>
      <p>
        We'll update this page when what we do changes, and tell you by email if it's significant. See
        also our <.link href={~p"/terms"} class="font-medium underline">Terms of use</.link>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layouts.app>
